 with  covid  the opening of schools there's a lot of  anxiety amongst voters and generally  they take it out on the politicians  while voters are still split on whether  to recall the governor  most about 40 percent are still  undecided on the pick to replace him  among the candidates the front runner  here remains republican radio talk show  host larry elder  now with 23 percent of voters saying  they would pick him up seven points from  just two weeks ago  that's where we see that huge number of  democrats truly undecided of what to do  nearly 70 percent  don't know who to vote for because  there's really not an alternative for  them  to choose our pollsters found governor  newsom is losing support from hispanic  voters  54 percent of latino respondents to this  poll said that they would vote to recall  the governor this is the only racial  group with the majority indicating that  they would do so  he's going to have to make a large  impact with that group of voters  now with that said he's really  struggling with less educated voters and  i think that's where the kogit  pandemic really separates the society so  if you don't have a college degree  you seem to be more upset and you seem  to want to recall the governor  with a college degree less the case you  want to keep the governor  to give you a breakdown of the party  affiliation of those who responded to  this poll  46 percent identified as democrat about  23 percent identified as republican  and about 30 percent said no party  preference  lake levels continue to drop at lake  mead amid a historic  mega drought in the west nevada senator  catherine cortez masto says she has a  plan to help  by recycling sewage water on a large  scale  our washington d.c correspondent  alexandra lamone explains  much of california's wastewater is  treated and dumped into the pacific  but as western states continue to suffer  through a mega drought  water managers and lawmakers say  wastewater must be recycled  recycled water is going to be extremely  important not just in the west  but likely throughout the entire nation  bronson mack with the southern nevada  water authority says  southern nevada already recycles all  indoor waste water  not just from sinks and tubs but from  toilets too  they use a process of biological and  chemical processes  multi-staged filtration and then a  disinfection process  portions of western states already  recycle their wastewater but because of  the drought  climate change and population growth  experts say  larger scale water recycling is a must  we have to figure out  how we manage not the water just for the  current population  but like our southern nevada water  authority does projects 50 years in the  future  nevada senator catherine cortez masto  introduced a bill to help pay for  large-scale water recycling projects to  turn  sewage water into drinking water for  example  there's an opportunity for nevada to  partner with california for california  to actually recycle sewage water senator  cortez master's bill  is co-sponsored by california senator  alex padilla  and is so far included in the senate's  bipartisan infrastructure bill  it would provide 450 million dollars in  grants to 17 western states for  large-scale water recycling projects in  washington  alexandra limon joining us to talk about  this bill is  alexandra lamone from our washington d.c  bureau  alex you mentioned the nevada california  partnership to recycle water in the los  angeles area that may be good news for  california but  what does nevada get out of that yeah  that's right this is a really great  partnership that's going to help  try to start recycling water in the los  angeles  area so that is a huge project but  southern nevada in return for  its investment in this project is  getting something out of it they will  get a share of that water  that is being recycled or that will be  recycled in the future  in the la area but instead of you know  having to pipe it and pump it back from  california  to nevada what's going to happen is that  nevada will get  an allotment that same allotment of that  water that they will instead now be able  to take  from lake mead from the colorado river  through lake mead  in return for that investment and you  said the water recycling bill is  included in the infrastructure package  tell us about the other  major water investments included in that  package  well in addition to just the lack of  water that we're seeing in the west  because of this ongoing mega drought  conditions  another big issue that the entire  country is facing  is access to clean and safe drinking  water in a lot of places  the water infrastructure the water pipes  are just so  old they're decades old sometimes 100  years old  and they are lead leaching pipes so they  contaminate the water  in other areas there's issues with  chemical contamination of water  um and things like pfos being in the  water so the  infrastructure bill actually also  includes tens of billions of dollars  to be able to be dispersed throughout  the country to help try to deal with  some of those water contamination issues  to also make sure that the water that is  available is actually safe for people to  drink  in what to watch here's what we're  keeping an eye on next week first up  lake mead levels a federal shortage is  expected to be declared along the  colorado river in the next few weeks  that could initiate cuts in both arizona  and nevada  we're waiting to see what else happens  with it plus monday is back to school  we're watching to see how mask mandates  and all classes are going  and if more steps will be necessary this  school year thanks so much for watching  politics now you can stay up to date  online  and watch us every saturday  [Music]  you
 

